# 🐳 Docker – Complete Interview Notes (With Command Explanations)

---

## 1. What is Docker?

Docker is a containerization platform used to build, ship, and run applications in isolated environments called containers.

* Lightweight
* Fast
* Shares host OS kernel

---

## 2. Physical vs VM vs Docker

| Feature  | Physical | VM             | Docker        |
| -------- | -------- | -------------- | ------------- |
| OS       | Separate | Full OS per VM | Shared kernel |
| Speed    | Slow     | Medium         | Fast          |
| Resource | High     | High           | Low           |
| Boot     | Slow     | Slow           | Very fast     |

---

## 3. Why Docker?

* Consistency (Dev = Prod)
* Fast deployment
* Lightweight
* Easy scaling

---

## 4. Docker Architecture

* Docker Engine → Core runtime
* Docker Daemon → Runs containers
* Docker CLI → User interface
* Docker Hub → Stores images

---

## 5. Key Concepts

**Image**: Blueprint/template used to create containers

**Container**: Running instance of an image

**Dockerfile**: Script containing instructions to build an image

---

## 6. Basic Commands

### Images

```
docker images
```

👉 Lists all images stored locally

```
docker pull ubuntu
```

👉 Downloads image from Docker Hub

```
docker rmi <image_id>
```

👉 Deletes an image

---

### Containers

```
docker run -it ubuntu bash
```

👉 Creates and starts a container interactively

* -i → interactive input
* -t → terminal
* bash → shell inside container

```
docker ps
```

👉 Shows running containers

```
docker ps -a
```

👉 Shows all containers (running + stopped)

```
docker stop <id>
```

👉 Gracefully stops a container

```
docker rm <id>
```

👉 Deletes a stopped container

---

## 7. Container Lifecycle

create → run → stop → remove

---

## 8. Important Commands

```
docker exec -it <id> bash
```

👉 Opens terminal inside running container (preferred over SSH)

```
docker inspect <id>
```

👉 Shows detailed information (IP, config, etc.)

```
docker top <id>
```

👉 Shows running processes inside container

---

## 9. Stop vs Kill

* docker stop → Graceful shutdown (SIGTERM)
* docker kill → Immediate stop (SIGKILL)

---

## 10. Networking

```
docker network ls
```

👉 Lists all networks

Types:

* bridge (default)
* host
* none

---

## 11. Port Mapping

```
docker run -p 8080:80 nginx
```

👉 Maps port 80 (container) to 8080 (host)
👉 Access via browser: [http://localhost:8080](http://localhost:8080)

---

## 12. Volumes

```
docker volume create myvol
```

👉 Creates persistent storage

```
docker run -v myvol:/data ubuntu
```

👉 Mounts volume inside container

---

## 13. Copy Files

```
docker cp file.txt <id>:/path
```

👉 Copies file from host → container

```
docker cp <id>:/path/file.txt .
```

👉 Copies file from container → host

---

## 14. SSH + SCP (Learning Only)

### Setup SSH

```
docker run -dit ubuntu
apt update
apt install openssh-server -y
passwd root
service ssh start
```

👉 Installs and starts SSH server

### Connect

```
ssh root@<container-ip>
```

👉 Login to container

### SCP

```
scp file.txt root@ip:/root/
```

👉 Copy file to container

```
scp root@ip:/root/file.txt .
```

👉 Copy file from container

⚠️ Not recommended. Use docker exec or docker cp.

---

## 15. Create Image

### docker commit

```
docker commit <id> my-image:v1
```

👉 Creates image from running container (snapshot)

### Dockerfile

```
FROM ubuntu
RUN apt update
CMD ["bash"]
```

👉 Defines steps to build image

### Build

```
docker build -t my-image:v1 .
```

👉 Builds image using Dockerfile

---

## 16. Push to Docker Hub

```
docker login
```

👉 Login to Docker Hub

```
docker tag my-image username/my-image
```

👉 Tags image with Docker Hub username

```
docker push username/my-image
```

👉 Uploads image to Docker Hub

---

## 17. Pull Image

```
docker pull username/my-image
```

👉 Downloads image from Docker Hub

---

## 18. Cleanup

```
docker system prune
```

👉 Removes unused data (containers, networks, images)

```
docker image prune
```

👉 Removes unused images

---

## 19. Run without sudo

```
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker
```

👉 Allows running docker without sudo

---

## 20. Image Layers

```
docker history <image_id>
```

👉 Shows image layers

---

## Interview Q&A

**Image vs Container**

* Image = template
* Container = running instance

**build vs commit**

* build = recommended (reproducible)
* commit = quick snapshot

**Why containers fast?**

* Share OS kernel

**Why no SSH?**

* Use docker exec

---

## Summary

* Docker = containerization tool
* Fast & lightweight
* Dockerfile is best practice
* Use volumes & networking in real projects
